<p>"What does it cost to live in Dubai?" depends enormously on neighborhood, lifestyle, and whether you have kids in private school. Below is a realistic breakdown by category, not a marketing-brochure estimate.</p>

<h2>Rent: The Biggest Variable</h2>
<p>Rent varies more by area than almost anything else in a Dubai budget:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Studio/1-bedroom, further-out areas</strong> (Dubai Sports City, Al Furjan, International City): typically the most affordable tier.</li>
  <li><strong>1–2 bedroom, mid-range areas</strong> (JVC, Business Bay, Al Barsha): moderate pricing, good transport links.</li>
  <li><strong>2–3 bedroom, premium areas</strong> (Downtown, Dubai Marina, Palm Jumeirah): significantly higher, often 2–3x the mid-range tier.</li>
</ul>
<p>Remember: most landlords expect 1–4 rent cheques per year, not monthly payments — budget accordingly using the reserve method in our <a href="/blog/how-to-budget-in-dubai">Dubai budgeting guide</a>.</p>

<h2>Utilities: DEWA and Chiller Fees</h2>
<p>DEWA bills swing hard by season — summer air-conditioning can push bills noticeably higher than winter months. If your building uses district cooling, check whether it's billed through DEWA or separately, since chiller fees are one of the most commonly underestimated costs for new residents.</p>

<h2>Groceries and Food</h2>
<p>Grocery costs depend heavily on where you shop — hypermarkets like Carrefour are typically more budget-friendly than boutique or delivery-only grocers. Food delivery apps (Talabat, Deliveroo, Careem) are convenient but carry markups and delivery fees that add up quickly if used several times a week — see our tips on <a href="/blog/saving-money-on-salik-dewa-delivery-apps">cutting delivery app spending</a>.</p>

<h2>Transport: Salik, Fuel, and Parking</h2>
<p>If you drive daily through toll gates, Salik charges accumulate into a real monthly line item — many residents are surprised when they actually total it up rather than treating it as background noise. Add fuel, parking, and Nol card top-ups for public transport, and transport easily becomes a top-5 budget category.</p>

<h2>School Fees</h2>
<p>For families, school fees are often the single largest annual expense after rent — frequently paid in one or two large installments per year. Treat this the same way as rent: divide the annual figure by 12 and reserve it monthly rather than treating installment-free months as extra income.</p>

<h2>Health Insurance</h2>
<p>Health insurance is mandatory in Dubai and typically employer-provided at a base tier, with better coverage available as a paid upgrade. Factor in out-of-pocket costs for dental, optical, and any coverage gaps not included in your employer's base plan.</p>

<h2>Putting It Together</h2>
<p>A realistic Dubai budget usually breaks down roughly like this as a share of take-home income: rent (largest single category), then a combined bucket of utilities + transport + groceries, then savings and discretionary spending. The exact split depends on your household size and whether you have school-age children — which is exactly why a single "cost of living" number is less useful than tracking your own real numbers from month one.</p>

